IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pid-dates`

-- Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) -- the questions of the person who makes a disclosure.
--
-- SEAT: the informant: the person (usually a public sector employee, but "any person", s 5(1)) who discloses public
-- interest information to a proper authority, and who may then suffer reprisal for it. "Is what I know public interest
-- information?", "To whom can I disclose it?", "Am I protected?", "Must it be investigated, and by when must I be told
-- what is being done?", "When can I go to a journalist?", "Can I sue for victimisation?", "Can I be moved away from the
-- people punishing me?", "Can my identity be revealed?", "Can I lose my protection?". The proper authority, the
-- Public Sector Commissioner, the Supreme Court, the employing authority and the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity get no
-- seat: their opinions and decisions are asked as facts.
--
-- Every exported question takes plain facts (yes/no, a date, a number, a choice from a list). No question takes a
-- DEONTIC or an EVENT. The proper authority's duty to notify within 3 months (s 10(1)) and the informant's duty to
-- supply requested information within the period specified (s 17(1)(a)) are regulative rules run inside questions.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 3 (public interest information, detrimental action; public authority, public officer and public
-- sector contractor as the person states them); Part 2 (ss 5, 6, 7A, 7, 8, 10, 11, 12); Part 3 (ss 13, 14, 15A, 15B, 15,
-- 16, 17, 18A); ss 24, 25. Out, with reasons, in NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA), Act No. 29 of 2003, compilation including the amendments up to the
-- Local Government Legislation Amendment Act 2016 (No. 26 of 2016), retrieved 7 September 2026, deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_29657.txt. The Public Interest Disclosure Regulations 2003 are not deposited
-- (prescribed classes and declared proper authorities, s 5(3)(i), and declared public authorities, s 3, are asked as facts).
--
-- Sourced from the Western Australian Legislation website, State of Western Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to someone else are INPUTS ("if the employing
-- authority is satisfied that ..."). Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an
-- answer that is the text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an answer where the text is limited by its own wording or
-- silent is marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003: the questions of the person who makes a disclosure`

§§ `s 3 -- Is it public interest information?`

-- s 3(1) "public interest information means information that tends to show that, in relation to its performance of a
-- public function ..., a public authority, a public officer, or a public sector contractor is, has been, or proposes to
-- be, involved in -- (a) improper conduct; or (b) ... an offence under a written law; or (c) a substantial unauthorised
-- or irregular use of, or substantial mismanagement of, public resources; or (d) ... a substantial and specific risk of
-- (i) injury to public health; or (ii) prejudice to public safety; or (iii) harm to the environment; or (e) a matter of
-- administration that can be investigated under section 14 of the Parliamentary Commissioner Act 1971". A "means"
-- definition: when it fails, the text's no. "improper conduct" is not defined in the Act (finding 1) and is asked.
DECLARE `Whom the information is about` IS ONE OF
    `a public authority: a department, a State agency or other organisation under the Public Sector Management Act 1994, a local government, a body set up for a public purpose by a written law or by the Governor or a Minister, or a body declared by regulations (s 3)`
    `a public officer: a Minister, a Parliamentary Secretary, a member of Parliament, a judicial officer, a police appointee, a public service officer, a member, officer or employee of a public authority, or another holder of public office (s 3)`
    `a public sector contractor: a person (not an employee) contracted to supply goods or services to, or perform a public function for, a public authority or the State, or a subcontractor or employee of such a person (s 3)`
    `only a private person or business with no public role and no public contract`

DECLARE `What the information tends to show` IS ONE OF
    `improper conduct (s 3 public interest information (a))`
    `an act or omission that is an offence under a written law (s 3 public interest information (b))`
    `a substantial unauthorised or irregular use of, or substantial mismanagement of, public resources (s 3 public interest information (c))`
    `an act or omission involving a substantial and specific risk of injury to public health, prejudice to public safety or harm to the environment (s 3 public interest information (d))`
    `a matter of administration the Ombudsman can investigate under s 14 of the Parliamentary Commissioner Act 1971 (s 3 public interest information (e))`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `Is it public interest information` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is public interest information (s 3), whether the conduct is past, present or proposed and whenever it happened (s 3, s 5(5)(a))`
    `no: it is not about a public authority, a public officer or a public sector contractor (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
    `no: it is not about the performance of a public function (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
    `no: it does not tend to show any of the kinds of wrongdoing listed (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`

@export Is what I know "public interest information" under the Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) (s 3)? It must tend to show that a public authority, public officer or public sector contractor, in performing a public function, is, was or proposes to be involved in improper conduct, an offence, substantial misuse or mismanagement of public resources, a substantial and specific risk to public health, public safety or the environment, or a matter of administration the Ombudsman can investigate.
GIVEN `whom` IS A `Whom the information is about` @desc Whom your information is about
      `in performing a public function` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct is in relation to that body's or person's performance of a public function (their public role or public contract work), rather than their private affairs
      `what it shows` IS A `What the information tends to show` @desc What your information tends to show they are, were or propose to be involved in
GIVETH AN `Is it public interest information`
`s 3 -- is it public interest information` `whom` `in performing a public function` `what it shows` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `whom` EQUALS `only a private person or business with no public role and no public contract`
        THEN `no: it is not about a public authority, a public officer or a public sector contractor (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
        IF NOT `in performing a public function`
         THEN `no: it is not about the performance of a public function (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
        IF `what it shows` EQUALS `none of these`
         THEN `no: it does not tend to show any of the kinds of wrongdoing listed (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: it is public interest information (s 3), whether the conduct is past, present or proposed and whenever it happened (s 3, s 5(5)(a))`

§§ `s 5(2) -- Is my disclosure an "appropriate disclosure"?`

-- s 5(2): "A person makes an appropriate disclosure of public interest information if, and only if, the person who makes
-- the disclosure -- (a) believes on reasonable grounds that the information is true; or (b) has no reasonable grounds on
-- which to form a belief about the truth of the information but believes on reasonable grounds that the information may
-- be true." "if, and only if": the text's no when neither holds. Whether the grounds are reasonable is for whoever later
-- decides it; the person states it.
DECLARE `What I believe about the information` IS ONE OF
    `I believe on reasonable grounds that it is true`
    `I have no reasonable grounds to form a belief about whether it is true, but believe on reasonable grounds that it may be true`
    `neither: I have no reasonable grounds to believe it is or may be true`

DECLARE `Is my disclosure appropriate` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you believe on reasonable grounds that it is true (s 5(2)(a))`
    `yes: you believe on reasonable grounds that it may be true (s 5(2)(b))`
    `no: a disclosure is appropriate if, and only if, you believe on reasonable grounds that the information is or may be true (s 5(2), the text's no)`

@export Is my disclosure an "appropriate disclosure" under the Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) (s 5(2))? It is if, and only if, you believe on reasonable grounds that the information is true, or, having no reasonable grounds to form a belief either way, you believe on reasonable grounds that it may be true.
GIVEN `my belief` IS A `What I believe about the information` @desc What you believe about whether your information is true, and whether you have reasonable grounds for it
GIVETH AN `Is my disclosure appropriate`
`s 5(2) -- is my disclosure appropriate` `my belief` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my belief`
    WHEN `I believe on reasonable grounds that it is true`
        THEN `yes: you believe on reasonable grounds that it is true (s 5(2)(a))`
    WHEN `I have no reasonable grounds to form a belief about whether it is true, but believe on reasonable grounds that it may be true`
        THEN `yes: you believe on reasonable grounds that it may be true (s 5(2)(b))`
    OTHERWISE `no: a disclosure is appropriate if, and only if, you believe on reasonable grounds that the information is or may be true (s 5(2), the text's no)`

GIVEN `my belief` IS A `What I believe about the information`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the belief makes it appropriate` `my belief` IF
    NOT (`my belief` EQUALS `neither: I have no reasonable grounds to believe it is or may be true`)

§§ `s 5(3), (4) -- To whom may I disclose it?`

-- s 5(3): "A disclosure of public interest information is made to a proper authority if -- (a) where the information
-- relates to an act or omission that constitutes an offence ... -- it is made to a police officer or to the Corruption and
-- Crime Commission; (b) ... public resources -- to the Auditor General; (c) ... a matter of administration ... -- to the
-- Parliamentary Commissioner or to a person who occupies a position specified under section 23(1)(a) in relation to the
-- public authority concerned; (d) ... a person who holds an appointment made under the Police Act 1892 ... -- to the
-- Commissioner of Police or to the Corruption and Crime Commission; (e) ... a judicial officer -- to the Chief Justice;
-- (f) ... a member of either House of Parliament -- to the Presiding Officer of the House ... to which the member belongs;
-- (g) ... a public officer (other than a member of Parliament, a Minister of the Crown, a judicial officer or an officer
-- referred to in Schedule 1 to the Parliamentary Commissioner Act 1971) -- to the Commissioner [the Public Sector
-- Commissioner, s 3] or the Parliamentary Commissioner; (h) ... a matter falling within the sphere of responsibility of a
-- public authority -- to a person who occupies a position specified under section 23(1)(a) in relation to that authority;
-- (i) ... a person or a matter of a prescribed class -- to a person declared by the regulations to be a proper authority
-- ...". s 5(4): where 2 or more paragraphs apply, any or all of their authorities. Each paragraph is a "where" condition:
-- when none holds for the person disclosed to, the text's no (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05). The regulations are not
-- deposited: (i) is asked as a fact.
DECLARE `Recipient` IS ONE OF
    `a police officer`
    `the Corruption and Crime Commission`
    `the Auditor General`
    `the Parliamentary Commissioner for Administrative Investigations (the Ombudsman)`
    `the officer designated under s 23(1)(a) to receive disclosures for the public authority concerned`
    `the Commissioner of Police`
    `the Chief Justice`
    `the Presiding Officer of the House of Parliament the member belongs to`
    `the Public Sector Commissioner`
    `a person the regulations declare to be a proper authority for information of this class`
    `a journalist`
    `anyone else (a manager, a member of Parliament, a union, the public)`

-- What the information relates to, as the person can state it. More than one may hold (s 5(4)).
DECLARE `What it relates to` HAS
    `an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
    `public resources` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a matter of administration` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a police appointee` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a judicial officer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a member of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
    `another public officer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a matter of a public authority` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a prescribed class` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `it` IS A `What it relates to`, `to` IS A `Recipient`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 5(3) -- made to a proper authority` `it` `to` IF
        (`it`'s `an offence` AND (`to` EQUALS `a police officer` OR `to` EQUALS `the Corruption and Crime Commission`))
    OR  (`it`'s `public resources` AND `to` EQUALS `the Auditor General`)
    OR  (`it`'s `a matter of administration`
            AND (    `to` EQUALS `the Parliamentary Commissioner for Administrative Investigations (the Ombudsman)`
                 OR  `to` EQUALS `the officer designated under s 23(1)(a) to receive disclosures for the public authority concerned`))
    OR  (`it`'s `a police appointee` AND (`to` EQUALS `the Commissioner of Police` OR `to` EQUALS `the Corruption and Crime Commission`))
    OR  (`it`'s `a judicial officer` AND `to` EQUALS `the Chief Justice`)
    OR  (`it`'s `a member of Parliament` AND `to` EQUALS `the Presiding Officer of the House of Parliament the member belongs to`)
    OR  (`it`'s `another public officer`
            AND (    `to` EQUALS `the Public Sector Commissioner`
                 OR  `to` EQUALS `the Parliamentary Commissioner for Administrative Investigations (the Ombudsman)`))
    OR  (`it`'s `a matter of a public authority`
            AND `to` EQUALS `the officer designated under s 23(1)(a) to receive disclosures for the public authority concerned`)
    OR  (`it`'s `a prescribed class` AND `to` EQUALS `a person the regulations declare to be a proper authority for information of this class`)

`every proper authority in s 5(3)` MEANS
    LIST `a police officer`
       , `the Corruption and Crime Commission`
       , `the Auditor General`
       , `the Parliamentary Commissioner for Administrative Investigations (the Ombudsman)`
       , `the officer designated under s 23(1)(a) to receive disclosures for the public authority concerned`
       , `the Commissioner of Police`
       , `the Chief Justice`
       , `the Presiding Officer of the House of Parliament the member belongs to`
       , `the Public Sector Commissioner`
       , `a person the regulations declare to be a proper authority for information of this class`

@export To whom may I make my disclosure so that it is made to a "proper authority" (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 5(3), (4))? Lists every proper authority for what your information relates to; where it relates to more than one thing, you may go to any or all of them. An empty list means none of s 5(3) applies.
GIVEN `an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information relates to an act or omission that is an offence under a written law
      `public resources` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a substantial unauthorised or irregular use, or substantial mismanagement, of public resources
      `a matter of administration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a matter of administration that the Ombudsman can investigate under s 14 of the Parliamentary Commissioner Act 1971
      `a police appointee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a police officer or other person appointed under the Police Act 1892 Part I, III, IIIA or IIIB
      `a judicial officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a judicial officer (a judge or magistrate)
      `a member of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a member of either House of the WA Parliament
      `another public officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a public officer who is not a member of Parliament, a Minister, a judicial officer or an officer listed in Schedule 1 to the Parliamentary Commissioner Act 1971
      `a matter of a public authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a matter within the sphere of responsibility of a public authority (a department, agency, local government or other public body)
      `a prescribed class` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a person or matter of a class prescribed by the Public Interest Disclosure Regulations, for which the regulations declare a proper authority
GIVETH A LIST OF `Recipient`
`s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` `an offence` `public resources` `a matter of administration` `a police appointee` `a judicial officer` `a member of Parliament` `another public officer` `a matter of a public authority` `a prescribed class` MEANS
    filter (GIVEN r YIELD `s 5(3) -- made to a proper authority` `it` r) `every proper authority in s 5(3)`
    WHERE
        `it` MEANS `What it relates to` WITH
            `an offence` IS `an offence`
            `public resources` IS `public resources`
            `a matter of administration` IS `a matter of administration`
            `a police appointee` IS `a police appointee`
            `a judicial officer` IS `a judicial officer`
            `a member of Parliament` IS `a member of Parliament`
            `another public officer` IS `another public officer`
            `a matter of a public authority` IS `a matter of a public authority`
            `a prescribed class` IS `a prescribed class`

DECLARE `Is this a proper authority` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a disclosure to this person is made to a proper authority for your information (s 5(3); s 5(4) where more than one paragraph applies)`
    `no: a journalist is not a proper authority; you may go to a journalist only in the cases s 7A(2) sets out, after disclosing to a proper authority`
    `no: this person is not a proper authority for what your information relates to (s 5(3), each paragraph a "where" condition: the text's no)`

@export Is the person I want to tell a "proper authority" for my information (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 5(3), (4))? Only a disclosure to a proper authority, matched to what the information relates to, is protected by s 13.
GIVEN `to` IS A `Recipient` @desc The person or body you want to disclose to
      `an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information relates to an act or omission that is an offence under a written law
      `public resources` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a substantial unauthorised or irregular use, or substantial mismanagement, of public resources
      `a matter of administration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a matter of administration that the Ombudsman can investigate under s 14 of the Parliamentary Commissioner Act 1971
      `a police appointee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a police officer or other person appointed under the Police Act 1892 Part I, III, IIIA or IIIB
      `a judicial officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a judicial officer (a judge or magistrate)
      `a member of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a member of either House of the WA Parliament
      `another public officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a public officer who is not a member of Parliament, a Minister, a judicial officer or an officer listed in Schedule 1 to the Parliamentary Commissioner Act 1971
      `a matter of a public authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a matter within the sphere of responsibility of the public authority whose designated officer you would tell
      `a prescribed class` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it relates to a person or matter of a class prescribed by the regulations, and the person you would tell is the one the regulations declare for it
GIVETH AN `Is this a proper authority`
`s 5(3) -- is this a proper authority for my information` `to` `an offence` `public resources` `a matter of administration` `a police appointee` `a judicial officer` `a member of Parliament` `another public officer` `a matter of a public authority` `a prescribed class` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `to` EQUALS `a journalist`
        THEN `no: a journalist is not a proper authority; you may go to a journalist only in the cases s 7A(2) sets out, after disclosing to a proper authority`
        IF elem `to` (`s 5(3) -- to whom may I disclose` `an offence` `public resources` `a matter of administration` `a police appointee` `a judicial officer` `a member of Parliament` `another public officer` `a matter of a public authority` `a prescribed class`)
         THEN `yes: a disclosure to this person is made to a proper authority for your information (s 5(3); s 5(4) where more than one paragraph applies)`
        OTHERWISE `no: this person is not a proper authority for what your information relates to (s 5(3), each paragraph a "where" condition: the text's no)`

§§ `ss 13, 17, 18A, 24(2), 5(6) -- Am I protected for making it?`

DECLARE `How I disclosed` IS ONE OF
    `to a proper authority under s 5`
    `to a journalist, in a case s 7A(2) allows`
    `to someone else`

DECLARE `Court relief from forfeiture` IS ONE OF
    `no order relieving me`
    `an order relieving me in whole`
    `an order relieving me in part`

DECLARE `Am I protected` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you incur no civil or criminal liability, and cannot be disciplined, dismissed, have your services terminated, or be held in breach of a duty of secrecy or confidentiality, for making the disclosure (s 13); your liability for your own part in what you disclosed is unaffected (s 6)`
    `partly: you forfeited the s 13 protection, but a court has relieved you of the forfeiture in part (s 17(2)); the order says how far`
    `no: you have forfeited the s 13 protection by failing without reasonable excuse to give requested information, or by disclosing the information otherwise than under this Act (s 17(1), the text's no); a court may relieve you if the failure or disclosure did not materially prejudice the public interest and is minor (s 17(2))`
    `no: a statement made knowing it, or reckless whether it is, false or misleading in a material particular is not protected by this Act (s 24(2), the text's no), and is an offence (s 24(1))`
    `no: nothing in this Act entitles you to disclose information subject to legal professional privilege (s 5(6), the text's no)`
    `no: it is not public interest information, so s 13 does not reach it (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
    `no: the disclosure is not an appropriate disclosure, because you do not believe on reasonable grounds that the information is or may be true (s 5(2), the text's no)`
    `uncertain: s 18A treats a disclosure to a journalist under s 7A(2) as a disclosure of public interest information for Part 3, but s 13 protects an appropriate disclosure to a proper authority under s 5 (NOTHING-kind; fork F4)`
    `s 13 protects only a disclosure to a proper authority under s 5; this Act gives no protection for a disclosure to anyone else, though other protections are kept (s 25) (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Am I protected from liability, discipline and dismissal for making my disclosure (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 13, with ss 5(6), 17, 18A, 24(2))? An appropriate disclosure of public interest information to a proper authority gives immunity from civil and criminal liability, discipline, dismissal and breach of confidentiality, unless the protection is forfeited or the statement was knowingly or recklessly false or misleading.
GIVEN `how` IS A `How I disclosed` @desc To whom you made the disclosure (ask the s 5(3) and s 7A questions first)
      `public interest information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information is public interest information (ask the s 3 question)
      `my belief` IS A `What I believe about the information` @desc What you believe about whether your information is true, and whether you have reasonable grounds for it
      `false or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you knew your statement was false or misleading in a material particular, or were reckless about whether it was
      `privileged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information would otherwise be subject to legal professional privilege
      `anonymous` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the disclosure anonymously
      `failed to assist` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed, without reasonable excuse, to give a person investigating the matter information they asked you for, in the manner and time they specified
      `disclosed elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you disclosed information contained in your disclosure otherwise than under this Act (for example to the media without meeting s 7A, or to colleagues)
      `relief` IS A `Court relief from forfeiture` @desc Whether a court has made an order relieving you from a forfeiture of protection under s 17(2)
GIVETH AN `Am I protected`
`s 13 -- am I protected for making my disclosure` `how` `public interest information` `my belief` `false or misleading` `privileged` `anonymous` `failed to assist` `disclosed elsewhere` `relief` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `how` EQUALS `to someone else`
        THEN `s 13 protects only a disclosure to a proper authority under s 5; this Act gives no protection for a disclosure to anyone else, though other protections are kept (s 25) (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF NOT `public interest information`
         THEN `no: it is not public interest information, so s 13 does not reach it (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
        IF `false or misleading`
         THEN `no: a statement made knowing it, or reckless whether it is, false or misleading in a material particular is not protected by this Act (s 24(2), the text's no), and is an offence (s 24(1))`
        IF NOT (`the belief makes it appropriate` `my belief`)
         THEN `no: the disclosure is not an appropriate disclosure, because you do not believe on reasonable grounds that the information is or may be true (s 5(2), the text's no)`
        IF `privileged`
         THEN `no: nothing in this Act entitles you to disclose information subject to legal professional privilege (s 5(6), the text's no)`
        IF `forfeited` AND `relief` EQUALS `no order relieving me`
         THEN `no: you have forfeited the s 13 protection by failing without reasonable excuse to give requested information, or by disclosing the information otherwise than under this Act (s 17(1), the text's no); a court may relieve you if the failure or disclosure did not materially prejudice the public interest and is minor (s 17(2))`
        IF `forfeited` AND `relief` EQUALS `an order relieving me in part`
         THEN `partly: you forfeited the s 13 protection, but a court has relieved you of the forfeiture in part (s 17(2)); the order says how far`
        IF `how` EQUALS `to a journalist, in a case s 7A(2) allows`
         THEN `uncertain: s 18A treats a disclosure to a journalist under s 7A(2) as a disclosure of public interest information for Part 3, but s 13 protects an appropriate disclosure to a proper authority under s 5 (NOTHING-kind; fork F4)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you incur no civil or criminal liability, and cannot be disciplined, dismissed, have your services terminated, or be held in breach of a duty of secrecy or confidentiality, for making the disclosure (s 13); your liability for your own part in what you disclosed is unaffected (s 6)`
    WHERE
        -- s 17(1)(a) does not apply to an anonymous informant (s 17(2A)); s 17(1)(b) does.
        `forfeited` MEANS (`failed to assist` AND (NOT `anonymous`)) OR `disclosed elsewhere`

§§ `s 6 -- Does disclosing excuse my own part in it?`

DECLARE `Does disclosing excuse me` IS ONE OF
    `no: making the disclosure does not affect your liability for anything the information relates to (s 6, the text's no)`
    `s 6 does not arise: you had no part in what you disclosed (NOTHING-kind)`

@export If I was myself involved in what I am disclosing, does making the disclosure excuse me from liability for it (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 6)? It does not: a disclosure does not affect the discloser's liability for anything to which the information relates.
GIVEN `involved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were yourself involved in the conduct your information is about
GIVETH A `Does disclosing excuse me`
`s 6 -- does disclosing excuse my own part in it` `involved` MEANS
    IF `involved`
    THEN `no: making the disclosure does not affect your liability for anything the information relates to (s 6, the text's no)`
    ELSE `s 6 does not arise: you had no part in what you disclosed (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `s 10 -- By when must I be told what is being done?`

DECLARE `Party` IS ONE OF `the proper authority`, `the informant`
DECLARE `Step` IS ONE OF
    `notify the informant of the action taken or proposed`
    `supply the information requested`

-- s 10(1): "If an appropriate disclosure of public interest information is made to a proper authority, that proper
-- authority must, subject to section 11, not more than 3 months after the disclosure is made, notify the person who made
-- the disclosure of the action taken or proposed to be taken in relation to the disclosure."
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`
`s 10(1) -- notify the informant within 3 months` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the proper authority`
    MUST `notify the informant of the action taken or proposed`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the proper authority` BECAUSE "the informant was not notified of the action taken or proposed within 3 months after the disclosure (s 10(1)); the informant may go to a journalist (s 7A(2)(d))"

-- Runs the s 10(1) rule. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH, so FULFILLED
-- means in time. Not exported; it takes no DEONTIC from outside.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 10(1) -- notifying on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 10(1) -- notify the informant within 3 months` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the proper authority` `notify the informant of the action taken or proposed` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export What is the last day by which the proper authority must tell me what action it has taken or proposes to take on my disclosure (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 10(1))? Not more than 3 months after the disclosure is made.
GIVEN `disclosed on` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the disclosure to the proper authority
GIVETH A DATE
`s 10(1) -- the last day to be told what is being done` `disclosed on` MEANS `disclosed on` `plus months` 3

DECLARE `Have I been told in time` IS ONE OF
    `told in time: the proper authority notified you within 3 months (s 10(1))`
    `not yet due: 3 months since your disclosure have not passed (s 10(1))`
    `the proper authority has not complied with s 10(1): 3 months have passed without notice of the action taken or proposed; you may now go to a journalist (s 7A(2)(d))`
    `s 10 does not apply: you disclosed anonymously (s 10(5), the text's no)`
    `s 10 does not apply to this authority: it deals with the matter, or reports progress, under another law (s 12(1), (4), the text's no)`
    `s 10 does not apply: the Chief Justice and the Presiding Officers are not proper authorities for ss 8 to 10 (s 7, the text's no)`

GIVEN `disclosed on` IS A DATE, `notified` IS A BOOLEAN, `notified on` IS A DATE, `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 10(1) has not been complied with` `disclosed on` `notified` `notified on` `today` IF
    IF `notified`
    THEN NOT (`s 10(1) -- notifying on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day notified`)
    ELSE (`s 10(1) -- the last day to be told what is being done` `disclosed on`) `is earlier than` `today`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the number of days from` `disclosed on` `until` (`s 10(1) -- the last day to be told what is being done` `disclosed on`)
        `the raw day` MEANS `the number of days from` `disclosed on` `until` `notified on`
        `the day notified` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

@export Have I been told in time what action is being taken on my disclosure (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 10(1), (5), ss 7, 12)? The proper authority must notify you within 3 months, unless you disclosed anonymously or another law governs how it deals with the matter.
GIVEN `disclosed on` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the disclosure to the proper authority
      `notified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proper authority has notified you of the action it has taken or proposes to take
      `notified on` IS A DATE @desc The day it notified you (if it has not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `anonymous` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the disclosure anonymously
      `chief justice or presiding officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you disclosed to the Chief Justice or to the Presiding Officer of a House of Parliament
      `another law governs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the authority is the Corruption and Crime Commission or the Ombudsman, or a body declared by regulations, dealing with the matter under its own law, or a declared body with its own duty to report progress to complainants (s 12(1), (4))
GIVETH A `Have I been told in time`
`s 10(1) -- have I been told in time` `disclosed on` `notified` `notified on` `today` `anonymous` `chief justice or presiding officer` `another law governs` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `chief justice or presiding officer`
        THEN `s 10 does not apply: the Chief Justice and the Presiding Officers are not proper authorities for ss 8 to 10 (s 7, the text's no)`
        IF `anonymous`
         THEN `s 10 does not apply: you disclosed anonymously (s 10(5), the text's no)`
        IF `another law governs`
         THEN `s 10 does not apply to this authority: it deals with the matter, or reports progress, under another law (s 12(1), (4), the text's no)`
        IF `s 10(1) has not been complied with` `disclosed on` `notified` `notified on` `today`
         THEN `the proper authority has not complied with s 10(1): 3 months have passed without notice of the action taken or proposed; you may now go to a journalist (s 7A(2)(d))`
        IF `notified`
         THEN `told in time: the proper authority notified you within 3 months (s 10(1))`
        OTHERWISE `not yet due: 3 months since your disclosure have not passed (s 10(1))`

§§ `s 10(2)-(4), s 11 -- What report can I ask for?`

DECLARE `What report can I get` IS ONE OF
    `a final report: as the investigation is complete, the proper authority that carried it out must give you a report of the outcome, any action taken or proposed, and the reasons (s 10(4)); it must leave out anything likely to affect a person's safety, an investigation of an offence, or another discloser's confidentiality (s 11)`
    `a progress report, at the authority's discretion: the investigation is not complete, and the authority may, but need not, report on its current status (s 10(3))`
    `ask first: you may ask the proper authority for a report on progress (s 10(2)); once asked, it must give a final report when the investigation is complete (s 10(4))`
    `a progress report as if your disclosure were a complaint under the law that governs this authority (s 12(4)(b)); s 10 does not apply to it (s 12(4)(a))`
    `none under this Act: s 10(2) to (4) do not apply to an anonymous disclosure (s 10(5), the text's no)`

@export What report on my disclosure can I ask for, and must I be given it (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 10(2)-(5), ss 11, 12(4))? You may ask for a progress report; while the investigation is open the authority may give one; once it is complete it must give a final report with the outcome, the action and the reasons.
GIVEN `anonymous` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the disclosure anonymously
      `asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked the proper authority for a report on progress
      `complete` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the investigation into the matter is complete
      `declared body with its own reporting duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the authority is a body declared by regulations that has its own duty under another law to report progress to complainants (s 12(4))
GIVETH A `What report can I get`
`s 10 -- what report can I get` `anonymous` `asked` `complete` `declared body with its own reporting duty` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `anonymous`
        THEN `none under this Act: s 10(2) to (4) do not apply to an anonymous disclosure (s 10(5), the text's no)`
        IF `declared body with its own reporting duty`
         THEN `a progress report as if your disclosure were a complaint under the law that governs this authority (s 12(4)(b)); s 10 does not apply to it (s 12(4)(a))`
        IF NOT `asked`
         THEN `ask first: you may ask the proper authority for a report on progress (s 10(2)); once asked, it must give a final report when the investigation is complete (s 10(4))`
        IF `complete`
         THEN `a final report: as the investigation is complete, the proper authority that carried it out must give you a report of the outcome, any action taken or proposed, and the reasons (s 10(4)); it must leave out anything likely to affect a person's safety, an investigation of an offence, or another discloser's confidentiality (s 11)`
        OTHERWISE `a progress report, at the authority's discretion: the investigation is not complete, and the authority may, but need not, report on its current status (s 10(3))`

§§ `s 8 -- Must my disclosure be investigated?`

DECLARE `Ground for refusing` IS ONE OF
    `none: the authority does not consider any ground in s 8(2) applies`
    `the matter is trivial (s 8(2)(a))`
    `the disclosure is vexatious or frivolous (s 8(2)(b))`
    `no reasonable prospect of sufficient evidence, because of the time since the matter happened (s 8(2)(c))`
    `the matter is being or has been adequately investigated by another proper authority (s 8(2)(d))`

DECLARE `Must it be investigated` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the proper authority must investigate it or cause it to be investigated (s 8(1))`
    `the authority may refuse to investigate, or stop investigating, on the ground it considers applies (s 8(2)); it must tell you its reason (s 8(3))`
    `the authority may refuse to investigate, or stop investigating, on the ground it considers applies (s 8(2)); as you disclosed anonymously it need not tell you why (s 8(4))`
    `not under this Act: the authority deals with the matter under its own law, and s 8(1) does not bind it (s 12(1), (3), the text's no)`
    `not under this Act: the Chief Justice and the Presiding Officers are not proper authorities for ss 8 to 10 (s 7, the text's no)`
    `s 8(1) does not require this authority to investigate: the disclosure does not relate to it, its officers or contractors, or a matter it has a function or power to investigate (NOTHING-kind; fork F6)`

@export Must the authority I disclosed to investigate my disclosure, and must it tell me if it refuses (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) ss 7, 8, 12)? It must investigate a disclosure about itself, its officers or contractors, or a matter it can investigate, unless it considers the matter trivial, the disclosure vexatious or frivolous, the evidence lost to time, or the matter already adequately investigated; if it refuses it must give you its reason, unless you were anonymous.
GIVEN `chief justice or presiding officer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you disclosed to the Chief Justice or to the Presiding Officer of a House of Parliament
      `relates to the authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the disclosure relates to the authority itself, a public officer or public sector contractor of it, or a matter or person it has a function or power to investigate
      `another law governs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the authority is the Corruption and Crime Commission, the Ombudsman, or a body declared by regulations, and the matter is one it deals with under its own law (s 12(1), (3))
      `ground` IS A `Ground for refusing` @desc Which ground, if any, the authority considers applies for refusing or discontinuing an investigation (its judgment)
      `anonymous` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the disclosure anonymously
GIVETH A `Must it be investigated`
`s 8 -- must my disclosure be investigated` `chief justice or presiding officer` `relates to the authority` `another law governs` `ground` `anonymous` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `chief justice or presiding officer`
        THEN `not under this Act: the Chief Justice and the Presiding Officers are not proper authorities for ss 8 to 10 (s 7, the text's no)`
        IF NOT (`ground` EQUALS `none: the authority does not consider any ground in s 8(2) applies`)
         THEN IF `anonymous`
              THEN `the authority may refuse to investigate, or stop investigating, on the ground it considers applies (s 8(2)); as you disclosed anonymously it need not tell you why (s 8(4))`
              ELSE `the authority may refuse to investigate, or stop investigating, on the ground it considers applies (s 8(2)); it must tell you its reason (s 8(3))`
        IF `another law governs`
         THEN `not under this Act: the authority deals with the matter under its own law, and s 8(1) does not bind it (s 12(1), (3), the text's no)`
        IF `relates to the authority`
         THEN `yes: the proper authority must investigate it or cause it to be investigated (s 8(1))`
        OTHERWISE `s 8(1) does not require this authority to investigate: the disclosure does not relate to it, its officers or contractors, or a matter it has a function or power to investigate (NOTHING-kind; fork F6)`

§§ `s 7A -- When may I go to a journalist?`

-- s 7A(2): "A person may make a disclosure to a journalist of substantially the same information that was the subject
-- of a disclosure of public interest information that the person has already made under this Act if the proper
-- authority ... (a) has refused to investigate, or has discontinued the investigation of, a matter raised by the
-- disclosure; or (b) has not completed an investigation ... within the period ending 6 months after the disclosure was
-- made; or (c) has completed an investigation ... but has not recommended the taking of action in respect of the matter;
-- or (d) has not complied with section 10(1) or (4), if applicable, in relation to the disclosure." The "if" fails: the
-- text's no. The information not yet disclosed under the Act: s 7A does not reach it (NOTHING-kind).

@export What is the last day of the 6 months within which the proper authority must complete its investigation, after which I may take the same information to a journalist (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 7A(2)(b))?
GIVEN `disclosed on` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the disclosure to the proper authority
GIVETH A DATE
`s 7A(2)(b) -- the last day of the 6 months` `disclosed on` MEANS `disclosed on` `plus months` 6

DECLARE `May I go to a journalist` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the proper authority refused to investigate, or discontinued the investigation (s 7A(2)(a))`
    `yes: the investigation was not completed within 6 months after your disclosure (s 7A(2)(b))`
    `yes: the investigation was completed but no action was recommended (s 7A(2)(c))`
    `yes: the proper authority has not complied with s 10(1) (notice within 3 months) or s 10(4) (a final report you asked for) (s 7A(2)(d))`
    `no: none of the cases in s 7A(2)(a) to (d) has arisen (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `s 7A reaches only substantially the same information you have already disclosed under this Act to a proper authority (NOTHING-kind)`

@export May I now take my information to a journalist (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 7A(2))? Only substantially the same information you have already disclosed under the Act, and only if the proper authority refused or stopped investigating, did not finish within 6 months, finished without recommending action, or did not notify you within 3 months or give a final report you asked for.
GIVEN `already disclosed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already disclosed this information under the Act to a proper authority, and what you would tell the journalist is substantially the same information
      `disclosed on` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the disclosure to the proper authority
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `refused or discontinued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proper authority (or the person it referred the matter to) has refused to investigate, or discontinued the investigation of, a matter raised by your disclosure
      `completed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the investigation has been completed
      `completed on` IS A DATE @desc The day the investigation was completed (if it has not been, any date; it is not used)
      `action recommended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If completed: whether the investigation recommended that action be taken on the matter
      `anonymous` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the disclosure anonymously
      `s 10 applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether s 10 applies to the authority (it does not for the Chief Justice, a Presiding Officer, or a body dealing with the matter under its own law: ss 7, 12)
      `notified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proper authority has notified you of the action taken or proposed
      `notified on` IS A DATE @desc The day it notified you (if it has not, any date; it is not used)
      `asked for a report` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the proper authority for a report on progress
      `final report given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, the investigation being complete, the authority has given you the final report s 10(4) requires
GIVETH A `May I go to a journalist`
`s 7A -- may I go to a journalist` `already disclosed` `disclosed on` `today` `refused or discontinued` `completed` `completed on` `action recommended` `anonymous` `s 10 applies` `notified` `notified on` `asked for a report` `final report given` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `already disclosed`
        THEN `s 7A reaches only substantially the same information you have already disclosed under this Act to a proper authority (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `refused or discontinued`
         THEN `yes: the proper authority refused to investigate, or discontinued the investigation (s 7A(2)(a))`
        IF `not completed within 6 months`
         THEN `yes: the investigation was not completed within 6 months after your disclosure (s 7A(2)(b))`
        IF `completed` AND (NOT `action recommended`)
         THEN `yes: the investigation was completed but no action was recommended (s 7A(2)(c))`
        IF `s 10 is applicable` AND (`s 10(1) breached` OR `s 10(4) not complied with`)
         THEN `yes: the proper authority has not complied with s 10(1) (notice within 3 months) or s 10(4) (a final report you asked for) (s 7A(2)(d))`
        OTHERWISE `no: none of the cases in s 7A(2)(a) to (d) has arisen (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    WHERE
        `the 6 months end` MEANS `s 7A(2)(b) -- the last day of the 6 months` `disclosed on`
        `not completed within 6 months` MEANS
            IF `completed`
            THEN `the 6 months end` `is earlier than` `completed on`
            ELSE `the 6 months end` `is earlier than` `today`
        -- s 10(5): s 10 does not apply to an anonymous disclosure, so (d) is not "applicable".
        `s 10 is applicable` MEANS `s 10 applies` AND (NOT `anonymous`)
        `s 10(1) breached` MEANS `s 10(1) has not been complied with` `disclosed on` `notified` `notified on` `today`
        -- s 10(4) fixes no period; a final report not yet given once asked for and complete counts (fork F5).
        `s 10(4) not complied with` MEANS `asked for a report` AND `completed` AND (NOT `final report given`)

§§ `s 17(1)(a) -- Must I help the investigation, and by when?`

-- s 17(1)(a): an informant who "fails, without reasonable excuse, to assist a person investigating a matter to which the
-- disclosure relates by supplying the person with any information requested ... in such manner, and within such period,
-- as is specified by the person making the request" forfeits the protection given by s 13. s 17(2A): not for an anonymous
-- informant.
GIVEN `the days specified` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`
`s 17(1)(a) -- supply the requested information within the period specified` `the days specified` MEANS
    PARTY `the informant`
    MUST `supply the information requested`
    WITHIN `the days specified`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the informant` BECAUSE "requested information not supplied within the period specified: without reasonable excuse, the informant forfeits the protection of s 13 (s 17(1)(a))"

GIVEN `the days specified` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 17(1)(a) -- supplying on this day is in time` `the days specified` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 17(1)(a) -- supply the requested information within the period specified` `the days specified`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the informant` `supply the information requested` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Did I help in time` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you supplied the information within the period specified (s 17(1)(a))`
    `not yet due: the period specified has not ended (s 17(1)(a))`
    `you supplied it late or not at all, but with a reasonable excuse, so you do not forfeit your protection (s 17(1)(a))`
    `you forfeit the s 13 protection: the information was not supplied within the period specified, without reasonable excuse (s 17(1)(a), the text's no); a court may relieve you if the failure did not materially prejudice the public interest and is minor (s 17(2))`
    `s 17(1)(a) does not apply: you disclosed anonymously (s 17(2A), the text's no)`

@export Have I helped the investigation in time, or have I lost my protection (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 17(1)(a), (2A), (2))? If the person investigating asks you for information, you must supply it in the manner and within the period they specify; failing without reasonable excuse forfeits your s 13 protection, unless a court relieves you.
GIVEN `asked on` IS A DATE @desc The day the person investigating asked you for the information
      `days specified` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days they gave you to supply it
      `supplied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have supplied the information in the manner they specified
      `supplied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you supplied it (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a reasonable excuse for not supplying it in time
      `anonymous` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the disclosure anonymously
GIVETH A `Did I help in time`
`s 17(1)(a) -- did I help the investigation in time` `asked on` `days specified` `supplied` `supplied on` `today` `reasonable excuse` `anonymous` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `anonymous`
        THEN `s 17(1)(a) does not apply: you disclosed anonymously (s 17(2A), the text's no)`
        IF `supplied` AND (`s 17(1)(a) -- supplying on this day is in time` `days specified` `the day supplied`)
         THEN `in time: you supplied the information within the period specified (s 17(1)(a))`
        IF (NOT `supplied`) AND (NOT (`the last day` `is earlier than` `today`))
         THEN `not yet due: the period specified has not ended (s 17(1)(a))`
        IF `reasonable excuse`
         THEN `you supplied it late or not at all, but with a reasonable excuse, so you do not forfeit your protection (s 17(1)(a))`
        OTHERWISE `you forfeit the s 13 protection: the information was not supplied within the period specified, without reasonable excuse (s 17(1)(a), the text's no); a court may relieve you if the failure did not materially prejudice the public interest and is minor (s 17(2))`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `asked on` `plus days` `days specified`
        `the raw day` MEANS `the number of days from` `asked on` `until` `supplied on`
        `the day supplied` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

§§ `s 15 -- Can I sue for victimisation?`

-- s 3(1) "detrimental action includes action causing, comprising, or involving -- (a) injury, damage, or loss; or
-- (b) intimidation or harassment; or (c) adverse discrimination, disadvantage, or adverse treatment in relation to a
-- person's career, profession, employment, trade, or business; or (d) a reprisal". An "includes" definition: other
-- harm is not excluded, and is answered NOTHING-kind.
DECLARE `What was done to me` IS ONE OF
    `injury, damage or loss (s 3 detrimental action (a))`
    `intimidation or harassment (s 3 detrimental action (b))`
    `adverse discrimination, disadvantage or adverse treatment in my career, profession, employment, trade or business (s 3 detrimental action (c))`
    `a reprisal (s 3 detrimental action (d))`
    `some other harm or disadvantage not listed`

DECLARE `Why it was done` IS ONE OF
    `because, or substantially because, I or someone else made, or intends to make, a disclosure of public interest information under this Act`
    `for some other reason`

DECLARE `Whom I would sue or complain about` IS ONE OF
    `the person who did or threatened it (the perpetrator)`
    `the perpetrator's employer`

DECLARE `Which route` IS ONE OF
    `proceedings in tort in a court (s 15(1))`
    `a complaint under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (s 15(4))`

DECLARE `Can I sue for victimisation` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is an act of victimisation, which you may sue for as a tort against the perpetrator (s 15(1), (2))`
    `yes: it is an act of victimisation, which you may sue for as a tort against the perpetrator's employer; the employer has a defence if it proves it was not knowingly involved, did not and could not reasonably have known, and could not by reasonable care have prevented it (s 15(2), (3))`
    `no: the employer has proved the defence in s 15(3) (not knowingly involved, did not and could not reasonably have known, could not by reasonable care have prevented it), so s 15 gives no claim against the employer on these facts (the text's no)`
    `yes: you may complain under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 as if it were an act unlawful under s 67 of that Act (s 15(4)); the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity may decline it if it has already been adequately dealt with by a competent authority (s 15(5))`
    `no: having lodged a complaint under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 about this act, you cannot then sue in tort for it (s 15(4), the text's no)`
    `no: having commenced proceedings in a court about this act, you cannot then complain under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (s 15(4), the text's no)`
    `no: s 15 has no retrospective operation, and no proceeding may be taken for an act before the Act commenced on 1 July 2003 (s 15(6), the text's no)`
    `no: it is an act of victimisation only if done because, or substantially because, of a disclosure under this Act (s 15(1), the text's no)`
    `perhaps: the harm is not one of the kinds listed in the definition of detrimental action, which "includes" them without closing the list (NOTHING-kind)`

`the day the Act commenced` MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY 1 7 2003

@export Can I sue, or complain to the Equal Opportunity Commissioner, because I was victimised for a public interest disclosure (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 15)? Taking or threatening detrimental action against someone because, or substantially because, anyone made or intends to make a disclosure is an act of victimisation: you may sue in tort (against the perpetrator or their employer), or complain under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984, but not both.
GIVEN `what` IS A `What was done to me` @desc What was done or threatened against you
      `why` IS A `Why it was done` @desc Why it was done or threatened
      `on` IS A DATE @desc The day it was done or threatened
      `against` IS A `Whom I would sue or complain about` @desc Whom you would bring the claim against
      `route` IS A `Which route` @desc Whether you would sue in a court or complain under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984
      `already took the other route` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already taken the other route about this act (lodged an Equal Opportunity complaint, if you now want to sue; or commenced court proceedings, if you now want to complain)
      `employer proves defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you sue the employer: whether the employer proves it was not knowingly involved, did not know and could not reasonably have known, and could not by reasonable care have prevented the act
GIVETH A `Can I sue for victimisation`
`s 15 -- can I sue for victimisation` `what` `why` `on` `against` `route` `already took the other route` `employer proves defence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `on` `is earlier than` `the day the Act commenced`
        THEN `no: s 15 has no retrospective operation, and no proceeding may be taken for an act before the Act commenced on 1 July 2003 (s 15(6), the text's no)`
        IF `why` EQUALS `for some other reason`
         THEN `no: it is an act of victimisation only if done because, or substantially because, of a disclosure under this Act (s 15(1), the text's no)`
        IF `what` EQUALS `some other harm or disadvantage not listed`
         THEN `perhaps: the harm is not one of the kinds listed in the definition of detrimental action, which "includes" them without closing the list (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `route` EQUALS `proceedings in tort in a court (s 15(1))` AND `already took the other route`
         THEN `no: having lodged a complaint under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 about this act, you cannot then sue in tort for it (s 15(4), the text's no)`
        IF `route` EQUALS `a complaint under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (s 15(4))` AND `already took the other route`
         THEN `no: having commenced proceedings in a court about this act, you cannot then complain under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (s 15(4), the text's no)`
        IF `route` EQUALS `a complaint under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (s 15(4))`
         THEN `yes: you may complain under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 as if it were an act unlawful under s 67 of that Act (s 15(4)); the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity may decline it if it has already been adequately dealt with by a competent authority (s 15(5))`
        IF `against` EQUALS `the person who did or threatened it (the perpetrator)`
         THEN `yes: it is an act of victimisation, which you may sue for as a tort against the perpetrator (s 15(1), (2))`
        IF `employer proves defence`
         THEN `no: the employer has proved the defence in s 15(3) (not knowingly involved, did not and could not reasonably have known, could not by reasonable care have prevented it), so s 15 gives no claim against the employer on these facts (the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: it is an act of victimisation, which you may sue for as a tort against the perpetrator's employer; the employer has a defence if it proves it was not knowingly involved, did not and could not reasonably have known, and could not by reasonable care have prevented it (s 15(2), (3))`

§§ `s 15A -- Can I get an injunction?`

DECLARE `Can I get an injunction` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Supreme Court, being satisfied, may order the person who took the detrimental action to remedy it, or grant an injunction in any terms it considers appropriate (s 15A(2))`
    `you may apply: the Supreme Court may make an interim order or grant an interim injunction until it decides (s 15A(1), (3)); a final order needs the Court to be satisfied the action was taken or intended in reprisal (s 15A(2))`
    `no order under s 15A(2): the Court is not satisfied that detrimental action was taken or intended in reprisal for a disclosure (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `s 15A is for a person who believes detrimental action has been or may be taken against them in reprisal for a disclosure under this Act (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I ask the Supreme Court to stop or undo a reprisal for my disclosure (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 15A)? A person who believes detrimental action has been or may be taken against them in reprisal for a disclosure may apply for an order to remedy it or an injunction, and interim relief while the application is decided.
GIVEN `I believe there is a reprisal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you believe detrimental action has been or may be taken against you in reprisal for a disclosure of public interest information under this Act
      `decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has finally decided your application
      `court satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If decided: whether the Court is satisfied that a person has taken or intends to take detrimental action against you in reprisal for the disclosure
GIVETH A `Can I get an injunction`
`s 15A -- can I get an injunction` `I believe there is a reprisal` `decided` `court satisfied` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `I believe there is a reprisal`
        THEN `s 15A is for a person who believes detrimental action has been or may be taken against them in reprisal for a disclosure under this Act (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF NOT `decided`
         THEN `you may apply: the Supreme Court may make an interim order or grant an interim injunction until it decides (s 15A(1), (3)); a final order needs the Court to be satisfied the action was taken or intended in reprisal (s 15A(2))`
        IF `court satisfied`
         THEN `yes: the Supreme Court, being satisfied, may order the person who took the detrimental action to remedy it, or grant an injunction in any terms it considers appropriate (s 15A(2))`
        OTHERWISE `no order under s 15A(2): the Court is not satisfied that detrimental action was taken or intended in reprisal for a disclosure (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

§§ `s 15B -- Can I be relocated away from the reprisal?`

DECLARE `My employment` IS ONE OF
    `a public service officer`
    `an employee employed in an organisation under the Public Sector Management Act 1994`
    `something else (a contractor, a local government employee, a private employee)`

DECLARE `Can I be relocated` IS ONE OF
    `yes: your employing authority must, as far as practicable, arrange to relocate you away from your existing work location, within your department or organisation or to another, but only with your consent to the specific arrangements (s 15B(3), (4))`
    `the arrangements need your consent: the employing authority may not relocate you without your consent to the specific arrangements it proposes (s 15B(4), the text's no)`
    `you may apply: apply in writing to your employing authority on the grounds in s 15B(2); it must arrange your relocation if satisfied the grounds are established (s 15B(2), (3))`
    `no duty to relocate: the employing authority is not satisfied that the grounds are established (s 15B(3), the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `s 15B(2) lets you apply only on both grounds: detrimental action in reprisal, and relocation the only practical means of removing the danger (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 15B is for public service employees only; other employees are not covered by it (NOTHING-kind); the principal executive officer of a public authority must protect its employees who disclose from detrimental action (s 23(1)(b))`

@export Can I be moved away from my workplace to protect me from reprisal for my disclosure (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 15B)? A public service employee may apply in writing to their employing authority, which must arrange relocation as far as practicable if satisfied that detrimental action has been or may be taken in reprisal and relocation is the only practical means of removing the danger, with the employee's consent.
GIVEN `my employment` IS A `My employment` @desc How you are employed
      `reprisal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether detrimental action has been or may be taken against you in reprisal for a disclosure of public interest information under this Act
      `only practical means` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether relocating you is the only practical means of removing, or substantially removing, the danger of a reprisal
      `applied in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied in writing to your employing authority for relocation
      `authority satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you applied: whether your employing authority is satisfied that the grounds are established
      `I consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you consent to the specific relocation arrangements the employing authority proposes
GIVETH A `Can I be relocated`
`s 15B -- can I be relocated` `my employment` `reprisal` `only practical means` `applied in writing` `authority satisfied` `I consent` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `my employment` EQUALS `something else (a contractor, a local government employee, a private employee)`
        THEN `s 15B is for public service employees only; other employees are not covered by it (NOTHING-kind); the principal executive officer of a public authority must protect its employees who disclose from detrimental action (s 23(1)(b))`
        IF NOT (`reprisal` AND `only practical means`)
         THEN `s 15B(2) lets you apply only on both grounds: detrimental action in reprisal, and relocation the only practical means of removing the danger (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF NOT `applied in writing`
         THEN `you may apply: apply in writing to your employing authority on the grounds in s 15B(2); it must arrange your relocation if satisfied the grounds are established (s 15B(2), (3))`
        IF NOT `authority satisfied`
         THEN `no duty to relocate: the employing authority is not satisfied that the grounds are established (s 15B(3), the text's no: its "if" fails)`
        IF NOT `I consent`
         THEN `the arrangements need your consent: the employing authority may not relocate you without your consent to the specific arrangements it proposes (s 15B(4), the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: your employing authority must, as far as practicable, arrange to relocate you away from your existing work location, within your department or organisation or to another, but only with your consent to the specific arrangements (s 15B(3), (4))`

§§ `s 16(1), (2) -- Can my identity be revealed?`

DECLARE `Can my identity be revealed` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you consented (s 16(1)(a))`
    `yes: it is made in accordance with an order of a court or other body with authority to hear evidence (s 16(1)(d))`
    `yes: it is made in accordance with s 152 or 153 of the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct Act 2003 (s 16(1)(f))`
    `yes: it is necessary for natural justice or to investigate the matter effectively, and you were advised beforehand of the disclosure and the reason (s 16(1)(b), (c), (2))`
    `yes, on the ground of natural justice or effective investigation (s 16(1)(b), (c)), but the person must first take all reasonable steps to tell you, a reasonable time before, that it will be made and why (s 16(2)), and has not`
    `yes: it is necessary for natural justice or to investigate the matter effectively (s 16(1)(b), (c)); as you disclosed anonymously, no advance notice is required (s 16(3A))`
    `no: no one may reveal information that might identify you as the person who made the disclosure; doing so is an offence, up to $24,000 or 2 years' imprisonment (s 16(1), the text's no)`
    `s 16(1) protects the identity of a person who made an appropriate disclosure; it does not reach this one (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can someone reveal that I am the person who made the disclosure (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 16(1), (2), (3A))? No one may reveal information that might identify you, unless you consent, it is necessary for natural justice or an effective investigation (with advance notice to you), a court orders it, or the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct Act allows it. Breach is an offence: up to $24,000 or 2 years.
GIVEN `appropriate disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made an appropriate disclosure of public interest information under this Act (ask the s 5(2) and s 3 questions)
      `I consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you consent to the disclosure of information that might identify you
      `necessary for natural justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether revealing it is necessary having regard to the rules of natural justice (for example, so that a person accused can answer)
      `necessary to investigate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether revealing it is necessary to enable the matter to be investigated effectively
      `court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is revealed in accordance with an order of a court or other person or body with authority to hear, receive and examine evidence
      `under the CCM Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is revealed in accordance with s 152 or 153 of the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct Act 2003
      `anonymous` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the disclosure anonymously
      `advised beforehand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person revealing it took all reasonable steps, a reasonable time before, to tell you it would be revealed and why
GIVETH A `Can my identity be revealed`
`s 16(1) -- can my identity be revealed` `appropriate disclosure` `I consent` `necessary for natural justice` `necessary to investigate` `court order` `under the CCM Act` `anonymous` `advised beforehand` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `appropriate disclosure`
        THEN `s 16(1) protects the identity of a person who made an appropriate disclosure; it does not reach this one (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `I consent`
         THEN `yes: you consented (s 16(1)(a))`
        IF `court order`
         THEN `yes: it is made in accordance with an order of a court or other body with authority to hear evidence (s 16(1)(d))`
        IF `under the CCM Act`
         THEN `yes: it is made in accordance with s 152 or 153 of the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct Act 2003 (s 16(1)(f))`
        IF `necessary for natural justice` OR `necessary to investigate`
         THEN IF `anonymous`
              THEN `yes: it is necessary for natural justice or to investigate the matter effectively (s 16(1)(b), (c)); as you disclosed anonymously, no advance notice is required (s 16(3A))`
              ELSE IF `advised beforehand`
                   THEN `yes: it is necessary for natural justice or to investigate the matter effectively, and you were advised beforehand of the disclosure and the reason (s 16(1)(b), (c), (2))`
                   ELSE `yes, on the ground of natural justice or effective investigation (s 16(1)(b), (c)), but the person must first take all reasonable steps to tell you, a reasonable time before, that it will be made and why (s 16(2)), and has not`
        OTHERWISE `no: no one may reveal information that might identify you as the person who made the disclosure; doing so is an offence, up to $24,000 or 2 years' imprisonment (s 16(1), the text's no)`

§§ `s 16(3), s 17(1)(b) -- May I reveal who my disclosure is about?`

DECLARE `May I reveal who it is about` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the person your disclosure is about consents (s 16(3)(a))`
    `yes: it is necessary to enable the matter to be investigated effectively (s 16(3)(b))`
    `yes: there are reasonable grounds to believe it is necessary to prevent or minimise the risk of injury to a person or damage to property (s 16(3)(d))`
    `yes: it is in accordance with an order of a court or other body with authority to hear evidence (s 16(3)(e))`
    `yes: it is in accordance with s 152 or 153 of the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct Act 2003 (s 16(3)(g))`
    `no: you must not reveal information that might identify the person your disclosure is about; doing so is an offence, up to $24,000 or 2 years' imprisonment (s 16(3), the text's no); and disclosing the contents of your disclosure otherwise than under this Act forfeits your s 13 protection (s 17(1)(b))`

@export May I tell others who my disclosure is about (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 16(3), s 17(1)(b))? No one, the discloser included, may reveal information that might identify the person a disclosure is about, unless that person consents, it is necessary for an effective investigation or to prevent injury or damage, or a court or the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct Act allows it. Breach is an offence: up to $24,000 or 2 years.
GIVEN `they consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person your disclosure is about consents to your revealing information that might identify them
      `necessary to investigate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether revealing it is necessary to enable the matter to be investigated effectively
      `to prevent injury or damage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there are reasonable grounds to believe revealing it is necessary to prevent or minimise the risk of injury to any person or damage to any property
      `court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is revealed in accordance with an order of a court or other person or body with authority to hear, receive and examine evidence
      `under the CCM Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is revealed in accordance with s 152 or 153 of the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct Act 2003
GIVETH A `May I reveal who it is about`
`s 16(3) -- may I reveal who my disclosure is about` `they consent` `necessary to investigate` `to prevent injury or damage` `court order` `under the CCM Act` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `they consent`
        THEN `yes: the person your disclosure is about consents (s 16(3)(a))`
        IF `necessary to investigate`
         THEN `yes: it is necessary to enable the matter to be investigated effectively (s 16(3)(b))`
        IF `to prevent injury or damage`
         THEN `yes: there are reasonable grounds to believe it is necessary to prevent or minimise the risk of injury to a person or damage to property (s 16(3)(d))`
        IF `court order`
         THEN `yes: it is in accordance with an order of a court or other body with authority to hear evidence (s 16(3)(e))`
        IF `under the CCM Act`
         THEN `yes: it is in accordance with s 152 or 153 of the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct Act 2003 (s 16(3)(g))`
        OTHERWISE `no: you must not reveal information that might identify the person your disclosure is about; doing so is an offence, up to $24,000 or 2 years' imprisonment (s 16(3), the text's no); and disclosing the contents of your disclosure otherwise than under this Act forfeits your s 13 protection (s 17(1)(b))`

§§ `s 24 -- Could my disclosure be an offence?`

DECLARE `My state of mind` IS ONE OF
    `I believe it is true, or may be true`
    `I know it is false or misleading in a material particular`
    `I am reckless about whether it is false or misleading in a material particular`

DECLARE `Is my disclosure an offence` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a statement to a proper authority purporting to be a disclosure, made knowing or reckless that it is false or misleading in a material particular, is an offence, up to $12,000 or 1 year's imprisonment, and is not protected by this Act (s 24(1), (2))`
    `s 24 does not make it an offence: you are neither knowing nor reckless about its being false or misleading (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 24 does not reach a statement that is not made to a proper authority (s 24(1a)) (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Could my disclosure itself be an offence (Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) s 24)? A statement to a proper authority purporting to be a public interest disclosure, made knowing it to be false or misleading in a material particular, or reckless about that, is an offence (up to $12,000 or 1 year) and loses the Act's protection.
GIVEN `my state of mind` IS A `My state of mind` @desc What you know about whether your statement is false or misleading in a material particular
      `to a proper authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement is made to a person who would be a proper authority for it under s 5(3) if it were a true disclosure
GIVETH AN `Is my disclosure an offence`
`s 24 -- is my disclosure an offence` `my state of mind` `to a proper authority` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `to a proper authority`
        THEN `s 24 does not reach a statement that is not made to a proper authority (s 24(1a)) (NOTHING-kind)`
        IF `my state of mind` EQUALS `I believe it is true, or may be true`
         THEN `s 24 does not make it an offence: you are neither knowing nor reckless about its being false or misleading (NOTHING-kind)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: a statement to a proper authority purporting to be a disclosure, made knowing or reckless that it is false or misleading in a material particular, is an offence, up to $12,000 or 1 year's imprisonment, and is not protected by this Act (s 24(1), (2))`

§§ `ss 14, 16, 24 -- The penalties`

DECLARE `Conduct` IS ONE OF
    `taking or threatening detrimental action against someone because of a disclosure (s 14(1))`
    `attempting, or inciting another, to take detrimental action because of a disclosure (s 14(2))`
    `revealing the identity of a person who made a disclosure (s 16(1))`
    `revealing the identity of the person a disclosure is about (s 16(3))`
    `making a false or misleading disclosure knowingly or recklessly (s 24(1))`

DECLARE `Maximum penalty` HAS
    `the most in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `the most years in prison` IS A NUMBER

@export What is the most a person can be fined or imprisoned for this conduct under the Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA) (ss 14, 16, 24)? Reprisal, attempting or inciting it, and revealing a discloser's or subject's identity: $24,000 or 2 years; a knowingly or recklessly false or misleading disclosure: $12,000 or 1 year.
GIVEN `the conduct` IS A `Conduct` @desc The conduct
GIVETH A `Maximum penalty`
`ss 14, 16, 24 -- the most a person can be punished` `the conduct` MEANS
    IF `the conduct` EQUALS `making a false or misleading disclosure knowingly or recklessly (s 24(1))`
    THEN `Maximum penalty` WITH `the most in dollars` IS 12000, `the most years in prison` IS 1
    ELSE `Maximum penalty` WITH `the most in dollars` IS 24000, `the most years in prison` IS 2
